e going to come  off as sympathetic or that some juror is  going to feel sorry for them or buy  their story now we had analyzed as a  team his statements repeatedly and we  had actually developed a  cross-examination that we rehearsed paul  actually played travis mcmichael and we  practiced it now of course as you know  no cross-examination plan survives  contact with the enemy and we had to  pivot because you never know where the  defendant's going to go you never know  what he's going to agree to what he's  not going to agree to and when he  disagrees with something you've said and  you know full well that that's a lie  you've got to be able to pull up the  transcript of his prior statements and  question him about those things  trouser michael's attorney jason  sheffield also spoke with law and crime  network he says his client was adamant  he must tell his side of the story  the first moment that we saw travis  there in a jail cell  right after he had been arrested and we  took on his representation  he was adamant that he needed to explain  himself  fortunately we felt the same way because  in a case such as this  where you have taken another man's life  and you've done so because you believe  that you yourself were about to die  you have to explain that fear you have  to explain that thinking to the jury and  so instantly we were connected on the  idea that travis was going to testify  it was our call that he testify first  and that he do so prior to any other  witness testifying on his behalf  because we did not want the jury to  think or feel that he had the benefit of  other witnesses  testifying about him or about his  experiences or about the neighborhood  adam you've had this conversation as a  defense attorney with a client whether  or not they should testify it's  absolutely their right but your thoughts  on trusting michael testifying in this  case  yeah i was just in rikers island  yesterday having this conversation  uh and it's a very tough one i think a  lot of attorneys are scared to put their  clients on and it is sort of rare but  i'm not scared of it and i don't think  other attorneys should be either and i  think what he said is correct it didn't  work but there was no way there's no way  for a jury to really get the sense of  what someone's feeling without them  saying it so in a case like this where  he's saying self-defense and it doesn't  look like self-defense you watch that  video it's visceral it does not look  like self-defense so if you're going to  try to convince a jury of that you got  to get up there you got to really put  yourself into it you got to really have  the emotion and and express the fear  that you were feeling so i think it made  a lot of sense even though you know  listen it's easy to quarterback a monday  morning quarterback and say oh it didn't  work it was a bad decision i don't think  it was i think it was the right call it  was just a tough evidence yeah and i  think to jason sheffield's credit as  well the order in which he wanted  trousers and michael to testify was also  very important you don't want to put him  on the back burner as a witness who's  heard all the other testimony from his  side and then is trying to say hey well  you heard what everyone else said this  is what i think as well so putting him  first and foremost especially before the  other defendants was pretty good uh at  least in my opinion terry looking at how  the prosecution prepared for this trial  does the result surprise you and was  there a part of their prep that stood  out to you  listen linda donakowski was amazing she  was the epitome of excellent lauren  nothing surprised me and it is no wonder  that when she came out of that trial she  got a hero's welcome the key to winning  any case is preparation and presentation  and donakowski did both she gathered  every single piece of evidence she and  her team they reviewed all of the  transcripts to make sure they knew what  travis mcmichael was g